{"name":"Wax Flower","hex":"#eeb39e","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"wax flower","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative form of waxflower."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/wax_flower"}],"entries":[{"title":"Artificial plants","blocks":[{"text":"Artificial plants, sometimes described as faux plants, are imitations of natural plants used for commercial or residential decoration. They are sometimes made for scientific purposes. Artificial plants vary widely from mass-produced varieties that are distinguishable from real plants by casual observation to highly detailed botanical or artistic specimens."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/Artificial_flowers_bouquet_oldfashioned.jpg/330px-Artificial_flowers_bouquet_oldfashioned.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_plants","wikidata":"Q163047"},{"title":"Chamelaucium","blocks":[{"text":"Chamelaucium, also known as waxflower, is a genus of shrubs endemic to south western Western Australia. They belong to the myrtle family Myrtaceae and have flowers similar to those of the tea-trees (Leptospermum). The most well-known species is the Geraldton wax, Chamelaucium uncinatum, which is cultivated widely for its large attractive flowers."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/df/Chamelaucium_uncinatum2.jpg/330px-Chamelaucium_uncinatum2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chamelaucium","wikidata":"Q142244"},{"title":"Wax","blocks":[{"text":"Waxes are a diverse class of organic compounds that are lipophilic solids that are malleable near ambient temperatures. They include higher alkanes and lipids, typically with melting points above about 40 °C (104 °F), melting to give low viscosity liquids. Waxes are insoluble in water but soluble in nonpolar organic solvents such as hexane, benzene and chloroform. Natural waxes of various types are produced by plants and animals and occur in petroleum."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ec/Beeswax_foundation.jpg/330px-Beeswax_foundation.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wax","wikidata":"Q124695"},{"title":"Flower","blocks":[{"text":"Flowers, also known as blossoms and blooms, are the reproductive structures of flowering plants. Typically, they are structured in four circular levels around the end of a stalk. These include: sepals, which are modified leaves that support the flower; petals, often designed to attract pollinators; male stamens, where pollen is presented; and female gynoecia, where pollen is received and its movement is facilitated to the egg. When flowers are arranged in a group, they are known collectively as an inflorescence."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/38/Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg/330px-Magnolia_grandiflora_-_flower_1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Flower","wikidata":"Q506"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/HK_SW_%E4%B8%8A%E7%92%B0_Sheung_Wan_%E5%B7%AE%E9%A4%A8%E4%B8%8A%E8%A1%97_Upper_Station_Street_CRIT_Room_green_plastic_leaves_September_2020_SS2.jpg/500px-HK_SW_%E4%B8%8A%E7%92%B0_Sheung_Wan_%E5%B7%AE%E9%A4%A8%E4%B8%8A%E8%A1%97_Upper_Station_Street_CRIT_Room_green_plastic_leaves_September_2020_SS2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c0/HK_SW_%E4%B8%8A%E7%92%B0_Sheung_Wan_%E5%B7%AE%E9%A4%A8%E4%B8%8A%E8%A1%97_Upper_Station_Street_CRIT_Room_green_plastic_leaves_September_2020_SS2.jpg/1280px-HK_SW_%E4%B8%8A%E7%92%B0_Sheung_Wan_%E5%B7%AE%E9%A4%A8%E4%B8%8A%E8%A1%97_Upper_Station_Street_CRIT_Room_green_pla